        MinOperations solver = new MinOperations();
        System.out.println("Ops for 39: " + solver.minOperations(39)); // Output: 3
    }
}



Two Strings form a complementary pair if there exists some permutation of their concatenation that forms a palndrome.
For example, the strings "abac" and "cab" form a complementary pair since their concatenation "abaccab" cab be rearranged to form the 
palndrome "bcaacb".
Given an array of strings, find the number of complementary pairs that can be formed. Note that pairs(i, j) and (i, j) are considered same.


import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class ComplementaryPairsHashMap {

    public static long countComplementaryPairs(String[] words) {
        // Map to store the frequency of each unique 26-character state string
        Map<String, Integer> signatureCounts = new HashMap<>();
        long totalPairs = 0;

        for (String word : words) {
            // Create an initial template array filled with '0' (all characters even)
            char[] state = new char[26];
            Arrays.fill(state, '0');

            // Compute the state signature for the current word
            for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
                int index = word.charAt(i) - 'a';
                // Pure conditional toggle: '0' becomes '1', '1' becomes '0'
                state[index] = (state[index] == '0') ? '1' : '0';
            }

            String currentSignature = new String(state);

            // Case 1: Look for an exact match (all combined characters will have even counts)
            totalPairs += signatureCounts.getOrDefault(currentSignature, 0);

            // Case 2: Look for states that differ by exactly one character
            char[] currentChars = currentSignature.toCharArray();
            for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
                // Temporarily flip the state of the i-th character
                char originalChar = currentChars[i];
                currentChars[i] = (originalChar == '0') ? '1' : '0';
                
                String targetSignature = new String(currentChars);
                totalPairs += signatureCounts.getOrDefault(targetSignature, 0);
                
                // Backtrack: Restore the character back to its original state
                currentChars[i] = originalChar;
            }

            // Record the current word's signature in our map
            signatureCounts.put(currentSignature, signatureCounts.getOrDefault(currentSignature, 0) + 1);
        }

        return totalPairs;
    }
}
























